WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.589
Toki: Vera, did chatbots always read like this?

00:00:02.989 --> 00:00:07.549
Vera: No. For decades, programs read text through a keyhole: one word at a time.

00:00:07.949 --> 00:00:09.667
Toki: And forgot most of what came before?

00:00:10.067 --> 00:00:13.258
Vera: Mostly. Each step in this story makes the keyhole wider.

00:00:14.858 --> 00:00:15.940
Toki: So what was the first step?

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:21.021
Vera: 2013: word vectors. Words become lists of numbers that capture meaning.

00:00:21.421 --> 00:00:23.799
Vera: King minus man plus woman lands near queen.

00:00:24.199 --> 00:00:26.138
Toki: Words with directions. Fancy.

00:00:27.738 --> 00:00:29.323
Toki: Then machines learned to translate?

00:00:29.723 --> 00:00:36.450
Vera: 2014: sequence to sequence. It squeezes the whole sentence into one vector, then writes the translation from it.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:38.372
Toki: A whole sentence in one vector?

00:00:38.772 --> 00:00:42.651
Vera: Exactly the problem. On long sentences, details get lost.

00:00:44.252 --> 00:00:45.508
Toki: So how do you stop the squeeze?

00:00:45.908 --> 00:00:51.777
Vera: Attention, also 2014. The decoder looks back at every word and picks which ones to focus on.

00:00:52.177 --> 00:00:53.296
Toki: No more single vector!

00:00:54.896 --> 00:00:55.853
Toki: And 2017?

00:00:56.253 --> 00:01:01.918
Vera: The transformer. It dropped recurrence and kept only attention, so it trains in parallel on GPUs.

00:01:02.318 --> 00:01:03.692
Toki: The T in ChatGPT?

00:01:04.092 --> 00:01:07.205
Vera: That's the one. And it kept improving as it was made bigger.

00:01:08.805 --> 00:01:09.964
Toki: Can I see the whole story?

00:01:10.364 --> 00:01:13.485
Vera: Pick one of the three acts on the timeline in the How it began chapter.

00:01:13.885 --> 00:01:16.532
Toki: Sixty years of ideas. I'll skim the highlights.
